# 8. Go package wildcards (`./...`, `dir/...`) are runnable, not elisions → pass
# (exit 0). Corpus calibration surfaced 16 such cells false-flagged before the
# Go-wildcard mask landed in Edit D; this pins the fix.
assert "v-go-wildcard" 0 "$FM"'
| # | What to verify | Test type | Timing | Test file / location |
|---|---------------|-----------|--------|---------------------|
| 1 | engine builds and tests pass | CLI-executable | post-impl | `cd engine && go build ./... && go test ./...` |
'

# 9. Survivor guard for case 8: a Go command whose `...` is an elided ARGUMENT
# (`--archive ...`), not a package wildcard, must STILL fire (exit 1). Without
# this, a future loosening of the Go-wildcard mask that also swallowed bare `...`
# args would pass silently. The mask keys on a `/` right before `...`, which this
# cell lacks, so it stays flagged — that boundary is what this case defends.
assert "v-go-wildcard-arg-elision" 1 "$FM"'
| # | What to verify | Test type | Timing | Test file / location |
|---|---------------|-----------|--------|---------------------|
| 1 | calibrate runs | CLI-executable | post-impl | `go run ./cmd/jsbcalibrate --archive ... --mode gate` |
'

# Message-content check (not just an exit code): gate V must name the offending
# cell text so an author knows which row to fix. Copies the `2>&1 | grep -q`
# idiom used for the [W] check above. Named without a `v-` prefix so the
# `grep -c '^ok: v-'` count stays at the 9 assert cases.
_f="$TMP/v-msg.md"
printf '%s\n' "$FM"'
| # | What to verify | Test type | Timing | Test file / location |
|---|---------------|-----------|--------|---------------------|
